---
// Audience-facing slide deck. Each of the 5 pitch sections is one focused
// slide: a short headline, 3–5 tight bullets, and one visual / canonical link.
// All speaker-only commentary lives in per-slide <aside class="notes"> blocks
// that are HIDDEN by default. Presenters reveal them with the "N" key or by
// loading the page with ?notes (so the notes view is shareable to a second
// screen). No sidebar — this is a present-mode surface.
import '../../styles/global.css';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const link = (p: string) => `${base}${p}`;
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="description" content="dot-agents founder pitch — present-mode slide deck. Press N for presenter notes." />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}favicon.png`} />
    <title>Pitch (present) — dot-agents</title>
    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        try {
          var t = localStorage.getItem('theme');
          if (t === 'light' || t === 'dark') {
            document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', t);
          }
        } catch (_) { /* localStorage unavailable */ }
      })();
    </script>
  </head>
  <body class="deck-body">
    <div class="deck">
      <div class="deck-bar">
        <a class="deck-home" href={link('/')}>← Full narrative</a>
        <span class="deck-hint">Press <kbd>N</kbd> for presenter notes</span>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== Slide 0 · Title ===== -->
      <section class="slide slide-title" id="s0">
        <div class="eyebrow">dot-agents — founder pitch</div>
        <h1>Run AI coding agents like operators, not chat windows</h1>
        <p class="slide-lede">One canonical config home. One operating loop the agents drive. Memory that compounds.</p>
        <aside class="notes">
          <strong>Presenter — opening (≈30s):</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>Hook: every team rebuilds the same agent context every single morning.</li>
            <li>Frame the deck: Problem → Approach → Actions → Results → Next, 5 slides.</li>
            <li>One sentence positioning: "dot-agents is the operating layer between you and any AI coding agent."</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== Slide 1 · Problem ===== -->
      <section class="slide" id="s1">
        <div class="slide-num">§1 · Problem</div>
        <h2>Configs sprawl every machine; context resets every session</h2>
        <ul class="slide-points">
          <li>5 platforms × N repos — sync is copy-paste, rules drift</li>
          <li>30–40 min/session re-explaining yesterday's work</li>
          <li>Agents rediscover what's broken, every session</li>
          <li>No safe way to run agents in parallel</li>
          <li>Tribal knowledge stays tribal; setup never improves itself</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="slide-link">
          <a href={link('/#problem')}>Full §1 — config + workflow fragmentation, 3 personas →</a>
        </p>
        <aside class="notes">
          <strong>Presenter — §1 (≈60s):</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>The real failure isn't the agent — it's the operational layer around it: config, state, memory, coordination.</li>
            <li>Name the 5 platforms explicitly if asked: Cursor (.cursor/rules), Claude Code (CLAUDE.md), Codex (AGENTS.md), Copilot (.github), OpenCode.</li>
            <li>Personas to invoke if the room is technical: solo multi-agent, small team shared standards, org with parallel agents.</li>
            <li>Land line: "Everyone builds half of this layer, badly, repeatedly."</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== Slide 2 · Approach ===== -->
      <section class="slide" id="s2">
        <div class="slide-num">§2 · Approach</div>
        <h2>Agent-as-operator, with structured boundaries</h2>
        <ul class="slide-points">
          <li>One canonical home — projected per platform, not copied</li>
          <li>Agent runs the loop; human owns <code>da review</code></li>
          <li>Approval gradient — auto-apply / propose / escalate</li>
          <li>Bounded delegation: write scope + structured merge-back</li>
          <li>Knowledge graph: notes + code graph + bridge</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="slide-link">
          <a href={link('/#approach')}>Full §2 — the four commitments →</a>
        </p>
        <aside class="notes">
          <strong>Presenter — §2 (≈75s):</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>Each commitment inverts a default that today's tools ship with.</li>
            <li>"Projected, not copied" is the load-bearing idea: edit once, every repo/agent picks it up on refresh.</li>
            <li>The inversion: before, humans drive the CLI; after, the agent drives it, humans approve via <code>da review</code>.</li>
            <li>If asked about parallelism risk: worktrees isolate filesystems, bundles isolate intent, orchestrator is the only thing crossing boundaries.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== Slide 3 · Actions ===== -->
      <section class="slide" id="s3">
        <div class="slide-num">§3 · Actions</div>
        <h2>What's shipped today, on master</h2>
        <ul class="slide-points">
          <li>The <code>da</code> CLI — config · workflow · resource · review</li>
          <li>Skills layer — orchestrator-session-start, loop-worker, iteration-close</li>
          <li>Hooks · <code>da kg</code> · MCP server surface</li>
          <li>The <code>pr-ci</code> verifier — shift-left CI ownership</li>
          <li>Fold-back + proposal loop; 34-entry lessons corpus</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="slide-link">
          <a href={link('/demos/demo_workflow_walkthrough/')}>Live walkthrough — a real wave, end-to-end →</a>
        </p>
        <aside class="notes">
          <strong>Presenter — §3 (≈75s):</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>Everything here is in the binary or the repo — reachable by a command you can run now.</li>
            <li>If demoing: open the workflow walkthrough; it traces PRs #118/#130/#133/#135.</li>
            <li>The verifier shift-left is the strongest single proof point — impl agent exits at merge-back, verifier owns the CI watch loop. Ties to §4.3.</li>
            <li>Skills sit between rules (always-on) and agents (subagents): on-demand procedures.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== Slide 4 · Results ===== -->
      <section class="slide" id="s4">
        <div class="slide-num">§4 · Results</div>
        <h2>Honest evidence — counted vs. traced</h2>
        <div class="slide-stats">
          <div class="stat"><span class="num">143</span><span class="lbl">merged PRs</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="num">61</span><span class="lbl">archived plans</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="num">34</span><span class="lbl">lessons</span></div>
        </div>
        <ul class="slide-points">
          <li>Repeat mistakes drop once a lesson lands as a rule</li>
          <li>Traceable: pattern → lesson → proposal → review → shipped</li>
          <li>Honest gap: token/wall-clock telemetry not yet instrumented</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="slide-link">
          <a href={link('/#results')}>Full §4 — the trace table + the honest caveat →</a>
        </p>
        <aside class="notes">
          <strong>Presenter — §4 (≈60s):</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>Lead with honesty — it earns trust. Counts are live-checked; the "3–5× cheaper / 30–40 min" figures are operator estimates, not telemetry.</li>
            <li>The traceable self-improvement loop is the most load-bearing claim, not the raw counts.</li>
            <li>If pushed on the gap: that's exactly what the observability platform (R2/R5 in §5) is being built to close. Don't oversell.</li>
            <li>dot-agents is its own primary case study — every PR went through the loop the product ships.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== Slide 5 · Next ===== -->
      <section class="slide" id="s5">
        <div class="slide-num">§5 · Next</div>
        <h2>Near, mid, far — each tied to an in-repo spec</h2>
        <ul class="slide-points">
          <li><strong>Near:</strong> layered PR fanout (spec accepted)</li>
          <li><strong>Mid:</strong> scoped knowledge graphs (spec accepted)</li>
          <li><strong>Mid:</strong> R3 background-worker service (plan-design)</li>
          <li><strong>Far:</strong> dream-cycle, two-tier config, R2/R5 observability</li>
          <li><strong>Not on roadmap:</strong> all-in-one UI, new runtime, default SaaS</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="slide-link">
          <a href={link('/#next')}>Full §5 — the near/mid/far timeline →</a>
          &nbsp;·&nbsp;
          <a href={link('/canonical/specs/layered-pr-fanout/')}>spec: layered-pr-fanout →</a>
        </p>
        <aside class="notes">
          <strong>Presenter — §5 + close (≈60s):</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>Every entry has an accepted spec or plan-design in the repo — nothing here is vapor.</li>
            <li>The "not on roadmap" list is deliberate: the agent is the UI; the dashboard is for review, not operation; local-first by default.</li>
            <li>Closing frame: "dot-agents is the substrate. Your rules, skills, plans, and memory are the product."</li>
            <li>Call to action: start with the workflow walkthrough, then the lessons narrative, then the canonical index.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>
    </div>

    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        // Presenter-notes visibility: hidden by default. Reveal via ?notes
        // (shareable URL) or the "N" key (live toggle). Persist the live
        // choice in sessionStorage so a reload mid-talk keeps the mode.
        var params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
        var on = params.has('notes') || sessionStorage.getItem('pitch-notes') === '1';
        function apply() {
          document.body.classList.toggle('notes-on', on);
        }
        apply();
        document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
          var tag = (e.target && e.target.tagName) || '';
          if (tag === 'INPUT' || tag === 'TEXTAREA') return;
          if (e.key === 'n' || e.key === 'N') {
            on = !on;
            try { sessionStorage.setItem('pitch-notes', on ? '1' : '0'); } catch (_) {}
            apply();
          }
        });
      })();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
